IPv4 vs IPv6:
-ipv6 can support authentication while ipv4 does not
-ipv6 can support encryption while ipv4 does not
-ipv6 supports something called quality of service which is used to classify packets in terms of priority

Some fields of the IPv4 header:
-protocol: 8 bits integer field that tells us what the current protocol that is encapsulated inside.
-ttl (Time to live): 8 bits integer fields that tells us how many router hops that the packet should travel at most and after that value it can no longer continue its journey. the packet after exceeding that amount will be expired. thus it will prevent loops and eventually will prevent DoS.
-fragmentation:
	-flags
	-fragment offset: simply the fragment number
the idea behind that is to divide the packet into several smaller packets in case the orignal packet is too big or in case the transportation medium is slow (i.e copper).

NOTE: the minimum IP header size is 20 bytes.

Some Fields of IPv6 Header:
NOTE: the length of the ipv6 header is fixed and it's 40 bytes.

-Traffic Class: serves as a priority class for the packet
-Flow Label
-Packet Length: is the same as the total length field in ipv4
-Next Header: is the same as the protocol field in ipv4
-Hop Limit: is the same as the TTL field in ipv4

IPv6 Features:
-Extended Address Space
-Auto Configuration: which allows the admin use the mac address as a part of the IP.
-Tunneling: sending ipv6 packets over ipv4
-Translating: sending ipv4 over ipv6
-Authentication


ICMP:
-a protocol that is peer to the IP protocol but it's a replacement for it, the main usage is for 2 things reporting errors on the network and finding if a certain host is a alive.
-2 common applications in use for the icmp protocol are traceroute and ping.

ICMP Header:
Code (1 Byte) and Type (1 Byte):
are used together to define specifically the condition of destination host with the reason for this condition.
for example type 3 code 3 means that the destination host is unreachable but because the port is not reachable, so that means that the host exists and we get this replay to a udp request that was sent to a specific host and port.

ICMP Message Types:
-Type 0 -> Echo Replay
-Type 5 -> Redirect
-Type 8 -> Echo Request
-Type 3 -> Desintation is unreachable:
	Code 0 -> Network Unreachable
	Code 1 -> Host Unreachable
	Code 3 -> Port Unreachable
	Code 9 -> Network Prohibited
-Type 11 -> Time Exceeded:
	Code 0 -> TTL Expired in transit
	Code 1 -> TTL Expired during ressembly



TCP:
a Connection Oriented protocol that guarantees the full delivery of data, one of the benificial features is flow of control which can be used by the server to either speed up or slow down the communication or in other words make clients either send smaller chunks or bigger chunks of data depending on its current state and it happens through the use of a field in the tcp header called "Window Size".

NOTE: a tcp connection can be either be closed gracefully or atruply

-in the 3 way handshake the client sends a ISN (initital sequence no) which is chosen randomly, the server then sends back an ack with ISN+1, then the client receives that and sends back an ack
-during the data transmisision the client takes the ISN and increaments it by the amount of bytes by currently sent in the current segment (the data size is determined via the window size field that was received previously by the other host) and then the server receives the segment takes the sq no and increaments by 1 and then sends it back to the client, then when the client receives the segment and finds out that the sq no received is the next sq no that it determined then it knows that the server received the segement successfully.

NOTE: minimum size of the tcp header is 20 bytes and more than that means that there are options.


Virtualization:
the idea is to run more than one guest os on the same hardware, it does that with the help of a software called "hybervisor" which uses the existing hardware to create virtual hardware that can be assigned the vms created.

NOTE: virtualization uses isolation to isolate vms from each other.


Virtualization Benfits:

1.Isolation of VMs: but the security risk here is if an app running on that vm is compromised then all other apps are too and the entire vm is also compromised, and to solve this issue we can apply application level isolation.

2.Disaster Recovery
at the end of the day a vm is a collection of files, so in terms of recovering a vm it's just a matter of copying this files back again from the last backup, and the power of this comes when a vm went down or got compromised we can easily recover it in a time reaches to 30 seconds, this is called MTTR (Mean Time to Recover).

VDI (Virtual Desktop Infrastructure): is a remote virtual desktop that is given to the employees rather than giving them actual laptops or pcs to work on.


3.Can be Used in Malware Analysis


Virtualization Risks:
1.what if the hybervisor got compromised then the attacker will have access to all vms as well as the host machine


2.Isolation Violation:
can lead "VM Escape" which can give the attacker access to the host machine.

VM Escapse: code that runs from the guest os but gets executed on the host os.


How to defend aganist the above security risks:
by installing patches for the hybervisor as soon as they arrive, by also doing hardening on these vms namely that we need to have the bair minimum services and applications running on that vm.

